Radium patch: optional ALSA xrun detection.

JUCE normally sets stop_threshold to the boundary, which disables the kernel's
XRUN detection completely: an underrun never produces -EPIPE, underrunCount
never increments, and getXRunCount() always returns 0 on Linux ALSA (see
JUCE issue #1741). When the "detect_alsa_xruns" preference is enabled, use the
hardware buffer size as stop_threshold instead (like JACK does), and count the
recovered xruns in the ALSA thread loop. Default is off.

Note: -EPIPE from snd_pcm_wait must not be passed to failed(). failed() calls
snd_strerror, which allocates, and Radium's DEBUG mode aborts/hangs (getchar)
if the realtime thread allocates. So -EPIPE is ignored there and handled via
the snd_pcm_avail_update recovery below.

commit 46d9c1aa16c7904f4cd301b788c37fc64e9f5beb
Author: Kjetil Matheussen <k.s.matheussen@notam02.no>
Date:   Thu Oct 8 17:37:35 2026 +0200

    Linux/Audio: Optionally detect xruns when not using Jack. Earlier the program gave the impression that it was detecting xruns when not using Jack, but it never did (JUCE doesn"t support it). By default it is turned off (to be backwards compatible), but it will probably turned on by default in the future. API: alsaXrunDetectionEnabled, setAlsaXrunDetectionEnabled

